\f[B]review\f[R]: Interactively review project files (see
\f[B]REVIEW\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]ruby\f[R]: List all ruby files in project or git (see
\f[B]RUBY\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]smell\f[R]: Classify method dependencies and smells.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]tc\f[R]: List custom unit tests (see \f[B]TC\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]test\f[R]: Run test suites (see \f[B]TESTING\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]todo\f[R]: Display TODO.md or scrape files for TODO comments (see
\f[B]TODO\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]update\f[R]: Update project files (see \f[B]UPDATE\f[R]).
.SH BUILD
.PP
Wile builds the gem package from the project gemspec into the working
directory (\f[V]<project>-<version>.gem\f[R]):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ wile build                    # Build gem package
$ wile build --force            # Build package even if checks fail
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Pre-build checks:
.PP
Before building, Wile verifies that:
.IP \[bu] 2
Git working directory is clean (\f[V]git status\f[R] shows no
uncommitted changes).
.IP \[bu] 2
No stale \f[V].gem\f[R] file exists in the working directory.
.IP \[bu] 2
The project gemspec exists and has been modified within the last 5
minutes.
.PP
Passing \f[V]--force\f[R] bypasses these verification checks and invokes
\f[V]gem build\f[R] immediately.
.PP
Options:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-f\f[R], \f[V]--force\f[R]: Force build even if Git status is
dirty, a stale gem package exists, or the gemspec has not been touched
recently.
.SH COMPARE
.PP
Wile compares project files against the living template in
\f[V]cache/\f[R], honoring \f[V].wileignore\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ wile compare                  # Compare all template files
$ wile compare README           # Show diff for matching files
$ wile compare lib/ test/       # Compare multiple paths
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Markers indicate file status:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]\[OK]\f[R] (green): Project file is identical to the template.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]\[*D]\f[R] (blue): Project file contains expected customizations
(such as \f[V]lib/\f[R], \f[V]bin/\f[R], \f[V]man/\f[R],
\f[V]README.md\f[R], \f[V]Gemfile\f[R], and dotfiles).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]\[u2717]\f[R] (red): Project file drifted from template outside
expected changes.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]# Missing:\f[R] (red): Template files absent from the project.
.PP
When invoked with one or more match patterns, \f[V]compare\f[R] displays
unified diff lines indented beneath each matching file.
Discrepancies are informational, so \f[V]compare\f[R] exits 0 on drift
or missing files.
.PP
Arguments:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]<match>...\f[R]: Optional patterns filtering files to compare and
diff.
.SH CONTENT
.PP
Wile inspects Ruby source files, displaying class, module, and method
definitions alongside their line counts and referenced dependency calls:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ wile content lib/foo.rb       # Inspect method line counts and calls
$ wile content lib/foo.rb --raw # Display raw line counts and definitions
$ wile content lib/foo.rb --dump # Dump parse tree as S-expressions
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Output details:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Method line counts\f[R]: Beside each method definition, Wile
displays the number of lines in the method (in green).
The count includes the \f[V]def\f[R] header and all body lines,
excluding only the closing \f[V]end\f[R] keyword.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Referenced dependencies\f[R]: Indented lines beneath a method show
calls and constants referenced within its body, color-coded by
resolution.
.PP
Arguments and options:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]<filename>+\f[R]: One or more Ruby source files to inspect.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]--raw\f[R]: Display plain method line counts and definitions
without color.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]--dump\f[R]: Dump internal S-expression AST structure to stdout
with source header on stderr.
.SH CP
.PP
Wile copies template files from the living template cache into the
project directory, keeping scaffolded files synchronized with upstream
enhancements:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ wile cp                       # Copy all missing template files
$ wile cp test                  # Copy template files matching pattern
$ wile cp .rubocop.yml --force  # Overwrite existing project file
$ wile cp --trace               # Show skipped and copied files
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Template behavior:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]ERB templates\f[R]: Template files ending in \f[V].erb\f[R] are
evaluated in the project context, replacing project placeholders and
removing the \f[V].erb\f[R] extension upon copying.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Safety\f[R]: Existing project files are skipped by default.
Pass \f[V]--force\f[R] to overwrite existing files with fresh template
copies.
.PP
Arguments and options:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]<match>\f[R]: Optional pattern filtering which template files to
copy.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-f\f[R], \f[V]--force\f[R]: Overwrite existing files in the
project.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-t\f[R], \f[V]--trace\f[R]: Trace execution, displaying skipped
files in grey and copied files in green.
.SH DICTIONARY
.PP
Wile provides trigram-based spell checking across \f[V]README.md\f[R],
\f[V]bin/*\f[R], and \f[V]lib/**/*.rb\f[R] via
\f[V]test/tc_dictionary\f[R].
Words not recognized by the trigram model are compared against the
\f[V].dictionary\f[R] whitelist:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ wile dictionary               # Check spelling and prompt to update
$ wile dictionary --check       # Check only; exit 1 on unrecognized words
$ wile dictionary --yes         # Auto-accept new words into .dictionary
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Options:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-c\f[R], \f[V]--check\f[R]: Run in check-only mode without
prompting.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-y\f[R], \f[V]--yes\f[R]: Answer yes to confirmation prompt
(auto-accept updates).
.SH GEMCHECK
.PP
Wile checks for missing gems in the currently running Ruby version
compared to installed versions managed by \f[V]rbenv\f[R].
By default, only the previous installed version is searched; passing
\f[V]--all\f[R] inspects all installed Ruby versions.
.PP
Major versions are tracked according to Semantic Versioning: for each
gem in the searched versions, each missing major version not currently
installed is reported with a pessimistic requirement (such as
\f[V]\[ti]> 1\f[R]):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ wile gemcheck                 # Check against previous Ruby version
$ wile gemcheck --all           # Check across all installed Rubies
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
If missing gems are found, Wile displays the current Ruby version, the
searched versions, and the missing gems, then prompts to install them
with their respective version constraints:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Current Ruby version: 4.0.7
Searching versions:   4.0.0
Gems missing in current found in other versions:
  reverse_markdown (\[ti]> 3):  4.0.0
  solargraph (\[ti]> 0):  4.0.0
Would you like to install them all? (Y/n)
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Options:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-a\f[R], \f[V]--all\f[R]: Search across all installed Ruby versions
instead of only the previous version.
.SH GEMFILE
.PP
Wile generates a project \f[V]Gemfile\f[R] directly from dependencies.
It classifies gems into runtime and development groups based on
\f[V].gemignore\f[R], including the rubygems source and
\f[V].ruby-version\f[R] constraint:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ wile gemfile                  # Generate or update project Gemfile
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The generated file follows standard Bundler syntax:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
source \[aq]https://rubygems.org\[aq]
ruby file: \[aq].ruby-version\[aq]
## runtime ##
gem \[aq]rake\[aq]
## development ##
gem \[aq]reek\[aq]
gem \[aq]rubocop\[aq]
\f[R]
.fi
.SH HELP
.PP
Wile scrapes task documentation directly from the header comments of
task source files in \f[V].tasks/*.rb\f[R] and
\f[V]lib/wile/tasks/*.rb\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ wile help                     # Show help for help and list tasks
$ wile help build               # Show detailed help for build task
$ wile help order review        # Show help for multiple tasks
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Arguments:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]<task>...\f[R]: One or more task names or partial names to inspect.
When omitted, Wile displays help for \f[V]help\f[R] and \f[V]list\f[R].
.SH INFO
.PP
Wile displays project metadata, description, and dependency
configuration synthesized from project dotfiles, \f[V]README.md\f[R],
and gemspec specifications:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ wile info                     # Display project metadata and gems
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Sections displayed:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Header\f[R]: Project name, version number, and author username.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Description\f[R]: Project summary extracted from the description
section of \f[V]README.md\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Files\f[R]: Complete listing of tracked project files packaged into
the gem.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Executables\f[R]: Executables located under \f[V]bin/\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Runtime gems\f[R]: Third-party gems and versions required at
runtime.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Development gems\f[R]: Dependencies required during development and
testing.
.PP
\f[V]wile info\f[R] takes no arguments and must be executed from the
project root.
.SH INSTALL
.PP
Wile builds the project gem and installs it into the local Ruby
environment using \f[V]gem install --no-document --local\f[R].
If the current version is already installed, it uninstalls it first
before installing the newly built gem, then cleans up the temporary
\f[V].gem\f[R] package file:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ wile install                  # Build and install gem locally
$ wile install --force          # Bypass git cleanliness and gemspec checks
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
By default, Wile enforces that the Git working tree is clean, no
leftover \f[V].gem\f[R] packages exist in the directory, and the gemspec
has been updated within the last 5 minutes.
Passing \f[V]--force\f[R] bypasses these verification checks.
.PP
Options:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-f\f[R], \f[V]--force\f[R]: Force installation without requiring
clean git status or up-to-date gemspec checks.
.SH LEXICON
.PP
Wile enforces vocabulary control across \f[V]lib/**/*.rb\f[R] using AST
parsing in \f[V]test/tc_lexicon\f[R].
Every method name, identifier, and symbol is tracked in
\f[V].lexicon\f[R] to prevent unintended API drift and subtle typos:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ wile lexicon                  # Inspect vocabulary drift and prompt
$ wile lexicon --check          # Check only; exit 1 on new vocabulary
$ wile lexicon --yes            # Auto-accept new vocabulary into .lexicon
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Options:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-c\f[R], \f[V]--check\f[R]: Run in check-only mode without
prompting.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-y\f[R], \f[V]--yes\f[R]: Answer yes to confirmation prompt
(auto-accept updates).
.SH LIST
.PP
Wile lists available built-in and custom tasks with optional pattern
filtering and multiple output formats:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ wile list                     # List all tasks with usage syntax
$ wile list dict lex            # Filter tasks matching patterns
$ wile list --format=name       # Print task names only
$ wile list --format=dictionary # Print dictionary-style task summaries
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Arguments and options:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]<match>...\f[R]: Optional patterns filtering task names.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]--format=WORD\f[R]: Output format (\f[V]USAGE\f[R],
\f[V]DICTIONARY\f[R], \f[V]NAME\f[R], or \f[V]CODE\f[R]).
Defaults to \f[V]USAGE\f[R].
.PP
Formats:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]USAGE\f[R]: Formatted task usage header with brief description
comment (default).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]DICTIONARY\f[R]: Concise list in \f[V]+ <task>: <description>\f[R]
format.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]NAME\f[R]: Bare task names only, suitable for shell completion and
scripts.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]CODE\f[R]: Unformatted usage comment lines extracted from task
source files.
.SH MKDIR
.PP
Wile creates a new Ruby gem project directory scaffolded from the living
template in \f[V]cache/\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ wile mkdir my_gem             # Create new project directory
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Arguments:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]<dirname>\f[R]: Exactly one argument specifying the new project
directory name.
Must contain only letters, numbers, underscores, or hyphens
(\f[V][a-zA-Z0-9_-]\f[R]).
.PP
Scaffolding steps:
.IP "1." 3
\f[B]Safety Check\f[R]: Verifies that \f[V]<dirname>\f[R] does not
already exist in the target path.
.IP "2." 3
\f[B]Template Copy\f[R]: Copies the template structure from
\f[V]cache/\f[R] into the new project directory.
.IP "3." 3
\f[B]Template Evaluation\f[R]: Evaluates all \f[V].erb\f[R] files (such
as \f[V]README.md.erb\f[R], \f[V]bin/project.erb\f[R], and
\f[V]lib/project.rb.erb\f[R]), substituting the project name, PascalCase
module name, Git author credentials, year, and date.
.IP "4." 3
\f[B]Path Renaming\f[R]: Renames template source and executable files
(e.g.
\f[V]bin/project\f[R] and \f[V]lib/project.rb\f[R]) to match the project
name, and deletes the \f[V].erb\f[R] templates.
.IP "5." 3
\f[B]Git Initialization\f[R]: Initializes a new Git repository
(\f[V]git init\f[R]) and installs template Git hooks (such as
\f[V].git/hooks/pre-commit\f[R]).
.SH NOTE
.PP
Wile scrapes project Ruby source files and prints every line containing
a \f[V]# NOTE\f[R] comment:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ wile note                     # Scrape NOTE comments in all Ruby files
$ wile note order               # Scrape NOTE comments in matching files
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Arguments:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]<match>...\f[R]: Optional patterns filtering Ruby file paths.
When omitted, all project Ruby files are inspected.
.SH NVIM
.PP
Wile opens the project\[cq]s standard files in Neovim, ordered by
internal dependency rank (\f[V]README.md\f[R], executable
\f[V]bin/*\f[R] scripts, then \f[V]lib/**/*.rb\f[R] in load order):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ wile nvim                     # Open all standard files in Neovim
$ wile nvim tasks               # Open files matching \[dq]tasks\[dq]
$ wile nvim --list              # List matching files without opening
$ wile nvim tasks --list        # List files matching \[dq]tasks\[dq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Arguments and options:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]<match>...\f[R]: Optional patterns filtering file paths.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-l\f[R], \f[V]--list\f[R]: Print file paths to stdout instead of
launching Neovim.
.SH ORDER
.PP
Wile analyzes call dependencies and method line lengths within a Ruby
source file to suggest an optimal top-to-bottom method order.
The optimization minimizes the line distance between callers and their
dependencies (preferring callees above callers) and breaks ties with
alphabetical ordering:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ wile order lib/foo.rb         # Suggest method order using default (bcmv)
$ wile order lib/foo.rb bprm    # Suggest order using best permutation
$ wile order lib/foo.rb dump    # Dump method data and index as JSON
$ wile order lib/foo.rb qc      # Check dependency integrity and cycles
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
When suggestions are found, Wile prints each method name alongside
movement indicators:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]\[ua]N\f[R] (arrow up): Method moved up by N positions from its
original place.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]\[da]N\f[R] (arrow down): Method moved down by N positions from its
original place.
.PP
Wile exits 0 when the file is already in optimal order, or 1 when
reordering is suggested.
.PP
Sort methods:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]bcmv\f[R] (default): Best combination move.
Evaluates relocating any method to any position (insert and shift),
committing the single best move per round until no move reduces total
distance.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]nswp\f[R]: Neighbour swap.
Fast first-improvement pass that sweeps forward and backward through
adjacent method pairs, swapping immediately upon any score improvement.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]cswp\f[R]: Combination swap.
First-improvement pass that tests all pairs \f[V](i, j)\f[R] and swaps
them immediately upon any score improvement.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]bnswp\f[R]: Best neighbour swap.
Evaluates all adjacent pairs \f[V](i, i+1)\f[R] and commits the single
best adjacent swap per round.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]bcswp\f[R]: Best combination swap.
Evaluates all possible pairs \f[V](i, j)\f[R] across the file and
commits the single best swap per round.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]bprm\f[R]: Best permutation.
Exhaustive search across a sliding window of 7 methods (5,040
permutations per step) sweeping forward and backward until convergence.
Reaches global optimum for files with <= 7 methods.
.PP
Special subcommands:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]dump\f[R]: Print parsed method definitions, sizes, and dependency
index as formatted JSON.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]index\f[R]: Print method call-site line offsets for each
dependency.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]isolate\f[R]: Print methods that neither call nor are called by
other methods in the file.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]total\f[R]: Print the file\[cq]s total valuation score with a
detailed trace of alphabetical and dependency distances.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]qc\f[R]: Quality check dependency symmetry and detect circular
dependencies.
Exits 65 (EX_DATAERR) if errors are found.
.PP
Arguments:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]<filename>\f[R]: Path to the Ruby source file to analyze.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]<sort-type|special>\f[R]: Optional sort algorithm or special
subcommand.
Defaults to \f[V]bcmv\f[R].
.SH REVIEW
.PP
Wile interactively reviews project files using \f[V]content\f[R],
\f[V]order\f[R], \f[V]reek\f[R], and \f[V]rubocop\f[R].
By default, it pauses after each file; passing \f[V]--all\f[R] enables
auto-advance mode, advancing through files that pass all checks and
stopping only when an issue or offense is detected.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ wile review                   # Review all lib files interactively
$ wile review --all             # Auto-advance, stopping only on errors
$ wile review gemcheck          # Filter to files matching gemcheck
$ wile review --only=rubocop    # Run only rubocop check
$ wile review --strict          # Use strict linter configurations
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Arguments:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]<target>\f[R]: Optional file path, directory path, or regex pattern
filtering the files to review.
When omitted, all project files in \f[V]lib/\f[R] are reviewed.
.PP
Options:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-a\f[R], \f[V]--all\f[R]: Auto-advance through passing files,
stopping only on errors.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-s\f[R], \f[V]--strict\f[R]: Use strict linter configurations
(\f[V].reek-strict.yml\f[R] and \f[V].rubocop-strict.yml\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]--only=CSV\f[R]: Comma-separated list of checks to run
(\f[V]content\f[R], \f[V]order\f[R], \f[V]reek\f[R], \f[V]rubocop\f[R]).
.SH RUBY
.PP
Wile lists all Ruby files in the project tracked by Git, including
\f[V].rb\f[R] source files and executable scripts with a Ruby shebang:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ wile ruby                     # List all project Ruby files
$ wile ruby tasks               # Filter files matching tasks
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Arguments:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]<match>...\f[R]: Optional patterns filtering file paths.
.SH TC
.PP
Wile discovers and lists test cases located in \f[V]test/tc_*\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ wile tc                       # List project custom unit tests
$ wile tc wile                  # List Wile template quality gates
$ wile tc all                   # List project and Wile tests
$ wile tc all --raw             # Suppress headers and spaces
$ wile tc help                  # Filter project tests matching \[dq]help\[dq]
$ wile tc all rubo              # Filter across all tests
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Arguments and options:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]<scope>\f[R]: Optional target category: \f[V]project\f[R]
(default), \f[V]wile\f[R] (template quality gates), or \f[V]all\f[R]
(both categories).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]<match>...\f[R]: Optional patterns filtering test file paths.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-r\f[R], \f[V]--raw\f[R]: Suppress section headings and blank lines
in \f[V]all\f[R] scope for piping to other commands.
.PP
When listing \f[V]project\f[R] tests, domain unit tests corresponding to
ranked \f[V]lib/\f[R] files (following the \f[V]tc_ut_<module>\f[R]
convention) are listed first in rank order, followed by any remaining
custom unit tests.
When \f[V]all\f[R] is selected without \f[V]--raw\f[R], tests are
grouped under \f[V]# Project:\f[R] and \f[V]# Wile:\f[R] headings.
.SH TESTING
.PP
Wile executes project test suites located in \f[V]test/**/tc_*\f[R].
The \f[V]test\f[R] task supports targeted filtering by test file and
test method name:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ wile test                     # Run all test suites
$ wile test --batch             # Run tests in parallel batch mode
$ wile test usage               # Run test files matching \[dq]usage\[dq]
$ wile test usage init          # Run test methods matching \[dq]init\[dq]
$ wile test --list              # List matching test files only
$ wile test -q                  # Fault-only output (suppress dots)
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Arguments and options:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]<filematch>\f[R]: Optional pattern filtering test file paths.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]<methodmatch>\f[R]: Optional pattern filtering test method names.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]--batch\f[R]: Run tests concurrently in a thread pool, suppressing
output.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-l\f[R], \f[V]--list\f[R]: Print matching test files without
running them.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-q\f[R], \f[V]--quiet\f[R]: Run with fault-only progress style.
.SH TODO
.PP
Wile displays project tasks from \f[V]TODO.md\f[R] or scrapes
\f[V]# TODO\f[R] comments from Ruby source files:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ wile todo                     # Display TODO.md items
$ wile todo order               # Scrape TODO comments in matching files
$ wile todo .                   # Scrape TODO comments across all files
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
When invoked without arguments, Wile displays \f[V]TODO.md\f[R]
formatted with \f[V]colora\f[R].
When invoked with one or more match patterns, Wile inspects project Ruby
files matching the patterns and prints their \f[V]# TODO\f[R] comments.
.PP
Arguments:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]<match>...\f[R]: Optional patterns filtering Ruby file paths.
When omitted, \f[V]TODO.md\f[R] is displayed.
.SH UPDATE
.PP
Wile synchronizes and regenerates project metadata files, keeping the
\f[V]README.md\f[R] version, copyright notice, active console output
blocks, gemspec specification, and man page documentation in sync:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ wile update                   # Update README, gemspec, and man page
$ wile update README            # Update only README.md
$ wile update gemspec           # Update only <project>.gemspec
$ wile update man               # Update only man page roff files
$ wile update --force           # Update despite uncommitted git changes
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
When updating \f[V]README.md\f[R], Wile synchronizes the first version
number with the current project version, updates the copyright year and
author, and refreshes any active console blocks.
Active console blocks are \f[V]console\f[R] code blocks whose first line
begins with \f[V]$ <project>\f[R] and ends with \f[V]#>>\f[R].
When \f[V]bin/<project>\f[R] exists, Wile executes the command and
replaces the block contents with the latest command output.
.PP
When updating the gemspec, Wile regenerates \f[V]<project>.gemspec\f[R]
from Git tracked files, runtime dependencies, development dependencies,
and the summary and description parsed from \f[V]README.md\f[R].
.PP
When updating man pages, Wile compiles \f[V]man/<project>.md\f[R] to
\f[V]man/<project>.1\f[R] using \f[V]pandoc\f[R] if available.
.PP
By default, Wile requires a clean Git working tree before applying
updates.
If changes are generated, Wile displays a git diff of the modified
targets and prompts to verify the changes.
.PP
Arguments:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V][README|gemspec|man]\f[R]: Optional target file to update.
When omitted, all three targets are updated.
.PP
Options:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-f\f[R], \f[V]--force\f[R]: Proceed with updates even if the Git
working tree has uncommitted changes.
.SH OPTIONS DICTIONARY
.PP
Wile maintains a canonical dictionary of CLI options and conventions.
When invoked with one or more options and no task, Wile queries this
dictionary:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ wile --format=json
--format=json
  Description: Output format
  Pattern:     WORD (/\[rs]A[\[rs]w-]+\[rs]z/)
  Commands:    rubocop, reek, cucumber
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
For each flag, the dictionary reports:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Description\f[R]: The standard purpose of the flag.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Pattern\f[R]: The type name and regular expression (for flags
taking a value).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Commands\f[R]: Established tools or tasks that use the flag.
.PP
Both active task flags and reserved flags for custom tasks can be
inspected.
If an unrecognized flag is passed or an option value fails pattern
validation, an error is reported.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\f[V]ruby\f[R](1), \f[V]fish\f[R](1), \f[V]tmux\f[R](1)
